(A hand-written program that is deliberately NOT deterministic across
 runs of the same binary - planted to prove the differential
 self-consistency oracle (FUZZER_DEFECTS.md defect 9) catches real
 nondeterminism, not just whatever the generator already proves clean.

 Printing raw /proc/self/stat is the honest way to construct this
 without a live compiler bug: its first field is this process's own
 pid, which differs every time the SAME binary is fork/exec'd again -
 exactly the "prints something that MOVES" case the oracle exists to
 catch. (Bug #39, the brief's own worked example, is fixed in the vox
 0.4.7 this repo now builds against, so its repro shape no longer
 reproduces - this is the brief's other suggested construction.))
open a file for reading called 'stat source' at "/proc/self/stat".
a buffer called 'stat data'.
Read from 'stat source' into 'stat data'.
Close 'stat source'.
a text called 'stat text' is 'stat data' as text.
Print 'stat text'.
